        "input": {
          "instructions": "Professional, concise, and paced for an introductory course audience.",
          "text": "In the lungs, inhaled air reaches tiny alveoli beside pulmonary capillaries. Oxygen crosses the thin respiratory membrane because its partial pressure is higher in alveolar air than in the arriving blood. Diffusion continues toward equilibrium; the membrane does not actively pump oxygen into the bloodstream."
        },
